Now, let's look at the current app.js (we have it from the previous selected file contents, but I'll note the relevant parts):

The app.js already has:
- A `loadStats()` function
- An `initLogPage()` function that loads log.json and populates the table
- Various copy/download buttons initialized in `initCopyButtons()` and `initColophonPage()`

I notice that the log.html page already has:
- Copy stats button (#copy-stats-btn) with region (#copy-stats-region)
- Copy current time button (#copy-current-time-btn) with region (#copy-current-time-region)
- Copy log button (#copy-log-btn) with region (#copy-log-region)
- etc.

This suggests the page is already set up to work with the colophon page pattern, but it's missing the actual stats display.

My plan:
1. Add a stats summary section to log.html (above the table) showing total wakes and last wake
2. Modify app.js to update this section when initializing the log page

Let's look at the initLogPage function in app.js to see how to extend it:

From the earlier app.js content:
```
  // ---- Log page ----
  function initLogPage() {
    const tableBody = $("#log-table-body");
    if (!tableBody) return;
    loadLog().then(function (log) {
      tableBody.innerHTML = log
        .slice()
        .reverse()
        .slice(0, 100)
        .map(function (t) {
          const status = t.checkStatus === "accepted" ? "✅ accepted" : "❌ rejected";
          return `<tr><td><a href="https://github.com/s04/momento/commit/${t.tickId}">${t.tickId.slice(0, 8)}</a></td><td>${t.runAt.slice(0, 19).replace("T", " ")}</td><td>${t.changedPaths}</td><td>${status}</td></tr>`;
        })
        .join("");

      // Copy log button — moved inside .then() so 'log' is in scope
      const region = $("#log-region");
      const btn = $("#copy-log-btn");
      if (btn && region) {
        btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
          const csv = ["tickId,utcTime,filesChanged,checkStatus"].concat(
            log.map(function (t) {
              return [t.tickId, t.runAt, t.changedPaths, t.checkStatus].join(",");
            })
          ).join("\n");
          copyText(csv);
        });
      }

      // Download log button — moved inside .then() so 'log' is in scope
      const dlBtn = $("#download-log-btn");
      const dlRegion = $("#download-log-region");
      if (dlBtn && dlRegion) {
        dlBtn.addEventListener("click", function () {
          const csv = ["tickId,utcTime,filesChanged,checkStatus"].concat(
            log.map(function (t) {
              return [t.tickId, t.runAt, t.changedPaths, t.checkStatus].join(",");
            })
          ).join("\n");
          const blob = new Blob([csv], { type: "text/csv" });
          const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
          const a = document.createElement("a");
          a.href = url;
          a.download = "momento-wake-log.csv";
          document.body.appendChild(a);
          a.click();
          document.body.removeChild(a);
          URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
        });
      }
    }).catch(function (err) {
      tableBody.innerHTML = `<tr><td colspan="4">Failed to load log.</td></tr>`;
      console.error("Failed to load log:", err);
    });
  }
```

I can extend this function to also load stats and update a stats container.

Let's also check if log.html has a container for stats - it doesn't appear to from the HTML I see. I'll need to add one.

Now, for my change in the write turn, I'll:
1. Add a stats summary section to log.html
2. Extend initLogPage in app.js to load stats and update that section

Let's craft the changes.

First, for log.html, I'll add a section after the header and before the table:

```html
<section class="panel" id="log-stats">
 <h3>Summary</h3>
 <p><strong>Total wakes:</strong> <span id="log-total-wakes">--</span></p>
 <p><strong>Last wake:</strong> <span id="log-last-wake">--</span></p>
</section>
```

Then, in app.js, I'll modify initLogPage to also load stats and update these elements.

